    "q-25489": {
      "dom_id": "step-q25489",
      "name": "Question 5",
      "problem": "Find the set of values of $x$ that satisfy $4x-5>9$ or $3<10x+8<16.$",
      "worked_solution": "We start by solving $4x-5>9:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x-5 & >9 \\\\ 4x & >14 \\\\ x & >\\frac{14}{4} \\\\ x & >\\frac{7}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14196049/assets/e39459442837c3de83da860526c5f39b048d02b33b6ea0a3340efd599bb880d3.png)\n\nThen, we solve $3<10x+8<16:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3 & <10x+8<16 \\\\ 3-8 & <10x<16-8 \\\\ -5 & <10x<8 \\\\ -\\frac{5}{10} & <x<\\frac{8}{10} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2} & <x<\\frac{4}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14196049/assets/611944fd64cf3638f0cfd4ae8f8a658496af927adc7b91f68e7aafdd36817272.png)\n\nTo graph the values of $x$ that satisfy either inequality, we overlay the two graphs on the same number line:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14196049/assets/112f50397f85c79e51650d88d2d4125cdb5479e60af579836bc3c8cd4eb22276.png)\n\nTherefore, the final solution is $x>\\frac{7}{2}$ or $-\\frac{1}{2}<x<\\frac{4}{5}.$ We can write this using interval notation as\n\n$$\n(-\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{4}{5})\\cup (\\frac{7}{2},\\infty ).\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
