    "q-28988": {
      "dom_id": "step-q28988",
      "name": "Question 6",
      "problem": "Find the set of values of $x$ that satisfy $2<4x-3<3$ or $4x-3\\ge 4.$",
      "worked_solution": "We start by solving $2<4x-3<3:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 & <4x-3<3 \\\\ 5 & <4x<6 \\\\ \\frac{5}{4} & <x<\\frac{6}{4} \\\\ \\frac{5}{4} & <x<\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14196049/assets/bac195a89ad5d09fa7753fe32bc5f4633453d37816c53330321e000579a8cb85.png)\n\nThen, we solve $4x-3\\ge 4:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x-3 & \\ge 4 \\\\ 4x & \\ge 7 \\\\ x & \\ge \\frac{7}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14196049/assets/09fd2be8420b15d39149183d5b802fdcdbd20739ab7364663e0592e61aaf12df.png)\n\nTo graph the values of $x$ that satisfy either inequality, we overlay the two graphs on the same number line:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14196049/assets/6ac5a412c535841b9872b1cbe4edee815e173dd43f3bb757616b7ca9a68200b8.png)\n\nTherefore, the final solution is $\\frac{5}{4}<x<\\frac{3}{2}$ or $x\\ge \\frac{7}{4}.$ We can write this using interval notation as\n\n$$\n(\\frac{5}{4},\\frac{3}{2})\\cup [\\frac{7}{4},\\infty ).\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices": [
            {
              "option": "a",
              "dom_id": "questionWidget-choiceLetterCircle-28988-a",
